<ContentLayout design="v2" structuredData={__sd} title="Keychron V6 Max Bluetooth not connecting: pairing and firmware checks | StackGeist" description="Reconnect a Keychron V6 Max by verifying mode and profile pairing, clearing stale host records, testing another device, and using firmware only as a last step." active="guides">
<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / Keychron V6 Max Bluetooth</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Peripheral troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Keychron V6 Max Bluetooth not connecting: pairing first, firmware last.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">The Keychron V6 Max supports three connection modes: wired USB-C, 2.4 GHz wireless, and Bluetooth. A Bluetooth failure can be a mode/profile issue, a stale pairing record on the host, low battery, host Bluetooth trouble, or firmware. Do the reversible pairing checks before flashing anything.</p>
<p class="credibility-note">Steps follow Keychron's official V Max troubleshooting and firmware guidance.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>Pair it again</strong><p>Switch the V6 Max to Bluetooth mode. Hold <strong>Fn + 1</strong>, <strong>Fn + 2</strong>, or <strong>Fn + 3</strong> for roughly 4–6 seconds, then select <strong>Keychron V6 Max</strong> on the computer. If that fails, forget the old keyboard entry on the host and retry another profile. Test the keyboard with a phone or second computer. Leave firmware flashing until the basic pairing path has been eliminated.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Confirm Bluetooth mode and enter pairing mode deliberately.</h2>
<p>Keychron’s current V Max troubleshooting directs users to select Bluetooth mode and hold <strong>Fn + 1/2/3</strong> for about 4–6 seconds. Use one profile at a time. The host should discover the device as <strong>Keychron V6 Max</strong>.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Delete the stale host record and pair again.</h2>
<p>If the keyboard was previously paired, remove the old Keychron entry from Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, or Android, toggle Bluetooth off and back on, and start a fresh pairing sequence. This changes the host state without modifying keyboard firmware.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Test another profile and another host.</h2>
<p>Try profile 2 or 3, then try a phone or second computer. If the keyboard pairs elsewhere, the keyboard’s Bluetooth radio is functioning and the first host becomes the main suspect. If no profile pairs with any host, continue with battery/reset/support steps.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Charge the keyboard and power-cycle it.</h2>
<p>Keychron’s V6 Max specification lists a 4000 mAh rechargeable battery. Charge it with a normal supported USB source, then switch the keyboard off and back to Bluetooth mode. Do not interpret a lit RGB backlight as proof that the battery state is adequate for stable wireless operation.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Use Keychron’s exact V6 Max reset/firmware path only if pairing still fails.</h2>
<p>Keychron maintains separate keyboard, Bluetooth, and receiver firmware. Match the firmware to the exact V6 Max layout/version. Keychron’s Bluetooth firmware page explicitly warns not to flash a working keyboard because the process can damage it. If you reach this step, follow the current V6 Max user guide or Keychron support rather than a firmware file copied from another model.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need a replacement keyboard?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If another profile or host works, solve the pairing/host Bluetooth issue.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If Bluetooth fails but 2.4 GHz and wired modes work, collect firmware versions and contact Keychron before replacing the board.</p></div>
<div><strong>Support / replacement</strong><p>If the keyboard cannot pair to multiple known-good hosts after Keychron’s documented reset/support procedure, a radio or board fault becomes more plausible.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Related</p><h2>If this did not solve it</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong><a href="/gear/input/keychron-v6-max/">Keychron V6 Max fit guide</a></strong><p>Size, switches, and connection modes, with reasons to skip it.</p></div>
